Use case: precise-object-edit / stylized-concept.
Create one polished still-image OBJECT DESIGN SAMPLE by editing the supplied Retro Sunset reference. This is a physical form/material exploration, not a recoloring exercise. Keep the approved composition, nearly square frame, dark burgundy-to-peach studio gradient, camera angle, generous empty top margin, text positions and all objects' approximate footprints. Keep all code unchanged and sharp, with its cream/cyan/lilac syntax colors on a calm dark background.
Keep THREE rear blue history cartridges and THREE front golden score tiles numbered 7, 2, 9. The same form and material must repeat exactly within each family. Each history cartridge contains fifteen equally-sized tightly-packed blue cards with distinct visible edges inside a navy tray, conveying stored items. Each cartridge retains ONE white 'history' label on a clean dark navy inset plaque. Each score tile retains ONE white 'score' label and one large ivory numeral, respectively 7, 2, 9, on a recessed DARK AMBER matte face. Warm buttery-gold sides must remain clearly golden; histories remain cobalt blue. White type must be highly readable with no glare. Strong warm/cool contrast. Soft contact shadows and tangible premium materials.
Make the redesigned silhouettes, bevels, layered faces and card construction visibly more tactile and appealing than the plain boxes/fins in the reference. Maintain the explanatory clarity and disciplined spacing. Scores remain in one tight front row and histories in the rear row. No extra props, wires, plugs, buttons, screws, empty slots, arrows, particles, banners, headings, palette names, mascots or decorative symbols. No additional words. No excessive glow.
The C++ text must remain exactly:
struct SplitPlayers {
  vector<int> scores;
  vector<array<int, 15>> histories;
};

for (int id : ids)
  total += scores[id];

Design direction A — SOFT ENAMEL TILES AND CARD CARTRIDGES. Golden tiles have generous softly rounded corners, wide convex golden bevels and smooth satin enamel sides; dark amber face is visibly recessed with a fine continuous inner seam. The score numerals look beautifully printed, not glowing. Rear history units become rounded navy cartridge trays containing fifteen cobalt cards with rounded top corners and softly chamfered edges. The cards look like solid laminated data cards, not metal cooling fins. Blue satin surfaces, bright cool edge highlights and deep navy separations. Dark label plaque integrates into the front rail of each cartridge. Premium tactile desktop-object aesthetic, cheerful and approachable, sculpted but clean.

